# Payload's generated import map, which must never reach a build.
#
# `layout.tsx` and the admin page import `./admin/importMap` with no extension,
# and webpack resolves `.js` before `.ts`. So whenever this generated sibling
# exists it *shadows* the tracked `importMap.ts` — the file everything in this
# repository assumes is the one being used, including the comment beside it in
# `.gitignore` and the assertions in `tests/design/import-map.test.ts`, which
# read the `.ts` and can therefore pass against a build that never loaded it.
#
# It is gitignored, so `git reset --hard` on the server does not remove it, and
# nothing in `.dockerignore` used to stop `COPY . .` from copying it in. One
# left on the VPS from an earlier `pnpm generate:importmap` is why the admin
# panel stayed blank through five deploys and a full `--no-cache` rebuild: every
# build was faithful, and every one of them compiled the stale map.
#
# Excluding it here means the image can only ever be built from the tracked
# file. Proven by building both ways: with the stale sibling present the bundle
# contains no `S3ClientUploadHandler` at all, and without it the component is
# there and the page chunk changes.
app/(payload)/admin/importMap.js
